<section class="subhero"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">SimpleTen / Sacramento, California</div><h1 data-reveal="words">AI consulting in Sacramento. Start with the right plan.</h1><p class="lede">You don’t need another list of AI tools. You need a clear view of where they can help your business, what implementation involves, and what to tackle first.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="#contact">Discuss your workflow <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="button secondary" href="/#blueprint">How the blueprint works</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section light"><div class="wrap article-grid"><div class="article-copy"><h2 data-reveal="focus">Your business first. The tools follow.</h2><p>SimpleTen works with Sacramento service businesses to turn broad questions about AI into a focused plan. We start with your customer journey, daily operations, and the handoffs that depend on someone remembering the next step.</p><h3 id="discovery">Find the work worth improving</h3><p>We review how inquiries arrive, how your team follows up, where information gets copied, and which tasks repeatedly interrupt people. We also identify work that should stay with a person. The result is a starting point based on your process, rather than a tool looking for a problem.</p><h3 id="priorities">Prioritize value and effort</h3><p>We consider frequency, time spent, customer impact, data quality, and implementation complexity. Together, we choose a manageable first workflow and the measures that will tell us whether it helps. A build is scoped separately so you can understand the work before committing.</p><h3 id="marketing">Connect marketing to the rest of the business</h3><p>Marketing OS brings content planning, search visibility, review requests, and reporting into the wider plan. We look at the path from discovery to inquiry and follow-up, so marketing activity connects to an accountable sales process. Search positions and lead volumes depend on more than a workflow and are not guaranteed.</p></div><aside class="scope-box"><h2>What we scope together</h2><ul><li>Current tools and workflow review</li><li>Bottlenecks and opportunities identified</li><li>Prioritized automation starting point</li><li>Human review and data-access requirements</li><li>Recommended measures of success</li><li>A scoped next step for implementation</li></ul><a class="button" href="#contact">Start with an assessment <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><small>Deliverables, timing, software costs, and ongoing support are agreed before a paid build begins.</small></aside></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap example-grid"><div class="example-copy"><span class="example-label">Example use case / illustrative</span><h2 data-reveal="words">An owner spending evenings catching up.</h2><p>A service-business owner checks multiple inboxes, copies inquiries into a spreadsheet, and reminds the team to follow up. A useful plan starts by understanding that routine and choosing one handoff to improve.</p></div><ol class="example-steps"><li>Map where each inquiry arrives and who owns it.</li><li>Identify information needed before the next step.</li><li>Choose a first workflow that fits the existing tools.</li><li>Compare lead response time and manual work before and after launch.</li></ol></div></section>
